## Run

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env
python manage.py makemigrations api
python manage.py migrate
python manage.py runserver 4300
```

## Layout

```
manage.py               # Django entry point (written once)
config/                 # project: settings, urls, wsgi, asgi (written once)
api/
  models.py             # re-exports the generated models (written once)
  urls.py               # includes the generated urls (written once)
  generated/            # owned by Backbone — regenerated wholesale, do not edit
    models.py           # DRF-backed models
    serializers.py      # validation
    views.py            # viewsets (only detected endpoints)
    urls.py             # router + auth routes
    permissions.py      # read-public / write-authenticated
<% if (auth) { %>    auth.py             # register / login / me (SimpleJWT)
<% } %>```

Migrations are Django's own — `makemigrations` derives them from the generated models. Anything
under `api/generated/` is overwritten on regenerate; everything else is yours.

